; Seatbelt profile (macOS `sandbox-exec`) denying network access to the
; process it wraps, while leaving everything else — reading and, where a
; caller separately allows it via Node's own permission model, writing files
; — untouched. Node's `--permission` flags have no network dimension at all
; (`node --help` lists `--allow-fs-read`, `--allow-fs-write`, `--allow-child-
; process`, `--allow-worker`, `--allow-addons`, and nothing for sockets), so
; the vm-escape mitigation in tools/check_clean_clone.sh needed a second,
; OS-level layer for the one thing Node's own model cannot restrict: a script
; that reaches `process` under the fs/child-process lockdown can still call
; `fetch()` or open a raw socket and quietly read a file's contents out over
; the network, which is a real concern next to a repository that (as of this
; scan) has a committed secret sitting in it.
;
; `(allow default)` first, then the one denial: this profile is not trying to
; be a general sandbox, only to close the one gap Node's model leaves open.
; `tools/sandbox_check.js` proves this holds by reproducing the exploit and
; attempting an outbound fetch from inside it, under this exact profile.
(version 1)
(allow default)
(deny network*)
